/*
We need the minimum possible median edge cost among all spanning trees
of a connected undirected graph with an even number of vertices n.

A spanning tree has n-1 edges. Since n is even, n-1 is odd, so the median
is the ((n-1)/2 + 1)-th smallest edge in the tree, i.e. the n/2-th smallest.

Let k = n/2.
For a threshold X, ask whether there exists a spanning tree whose median <= X.
That means the tree contains at least k edges with cost <= X.

Consider all edges with cost <= X. Let c be the number of connected components
in the graph formed by those edges.
Inside each such component, any spanning forest edge is "cheap" (<= X).
To connect all n vertices using as many cheap edges as possible, we can take
a spanning forest of each component, giving n - c cheap edges total, and then
use expensive edges to connect the c components together.

Thus the maximum possible number of cheap edges in any spanning tree is n - c.
So a spanning tree with at least k cheap edges exists iff:
    n - c >= k   <=>   c <= n - k = n/2

Since n is even and k = n/2, condition is simply c <= n/2.

Therefore, for each dataset, sort distinct edge costs and binary search the
smallest X such that the subgraph of edges with cost <= X has at most n/2
connected components.

Input format is not explicitly stated, but this classic problem usually uses:
multiple datasets, each begins with n m, terminated by 0 0.
*/

struct Edge {
    int u, v, w;
};

struct DSU {
    vector<int> p, r;
    int comps;
    DSU(int n = 0) { init(n); }
    void init(int n) {
        p.resize(n + 1);
        r.assign(n + 1, 0);
        iota(p.begin(), p.end(), 0);
        comps = n;
    }
    int find(int x) {
        if (p[x] == x) return x;
        return p[x] = find(p[x]);
    }
    bool unite(int a, int b) {
        a = find(a); b = find(b);
        if (a == b) return false;
        if (r[a] < r[b]) swap(a, b);
        p[b] = a;
        if (r[a] == r[b]) r[a]++;
        comps--;
        return true;
    }
};

int main() {
